Therefore Maier’s order is:

[2,5,\;6,3,\;4,1]

On a six-position wheel, those are three antipodal pairs:

\boxed{2\leftrightarrow5,\qquad6\leftrightarrow3,\qquad4\leftrightarrow1}

Every pair differs by exactly three positions—half a six-unit wheel.

That is extremely unlikely to be produced merely by “using Farmer’s music.” Maier had Farmer’s sequence:

[1,2,3,4,5,6]

but republished it as:

\boxed{[2,5]\,[6,3]\,[4,1]}

The operation is therefore not simple reversal. It is:

\boxed{\text{antipodal pairing followed by a reordered traversal of the three axes}}

What the three pairs do

XLV–XLVI: inversion → reciprocal convergence

XLV/ Farmer 35 says:

\textit{vertendo Bassum}

Turn the bass, begin from the beginning in d. Its discourse then supplies an actual cosmological wheel:

begin as the Sun turns in Capricorn;

the first operation finishes at Aries;

the “woman’s work” continues to Leo;

proceed until the year joins head to tail like a serpent.

XLVI/Farmer 38 immediately changes to:

\textit{Reciproca}

with two agents dispatched toward opposite horizons and returning simultaneously to the common center, Delphi. XLV text, XLVI text

So your proposed first pair is confirmed more strongly than we thought:

\boxed{ \text{turn one orientation} \rightarrow \text{execute its reciprocal from the antipode} \rightarrow \text{recover the center} }

XLVII–XLVIII: opposed conjunction → octave-level restoration

XLVII repeats the East–West construction, now as Wolf and Dog coming from opposite horizons and biting one another. Its musical instruction is:

\textit{in 8. duplici suprà}

—a double canon at the octave above.

XLVIII follows with:

\textit{in 5. seu 12. suprà,\ vertendo simplicem}

—at the fifth or twelfth above, turning the simplex or source voice. Its king repeatedly drinks water, becomes diseased/swollen, is purged, and is restored. XLVII, XLVIII

Thus the second pair appears to describe:

\boxed{ \text{opposed substances join} \rightarrow \text{octave/register elevation} \rightarrow \text{invert the generating source} \rightarrow \text{restore the transformed body} }

That is remarkably close to your boundary operation being a radix-changing octave realization rather than one more ordinary sibling division.

XLIX–L: generation → terminal mutual closure

XLIX is even more explicit:

the Philosophical Child has three fathers;

musically, turn the bass/fleeing voice;

start that voice at the beginning in d;

turn the source/simple voice;

start it at the end in g.

So the two transformed voices are assigned opposite textual endpoints:

\boxed{d_{\text{beginning}}\longleftrightarrow g_{\text{end}}}

That is the cleanest assembly instruction yet: turn both participating terms, but enter them from opposite ends.

Emblem L then closes the book through mutual destruction: Dragon and Woman kill one another, are joined face-to-face in one grave, and become covered in the same blood. Its epigram calls this “the true path of your work.” XLIX, L

Therefore:

\boxed{ \text{three-parent generation} \rightarrow \text{opposite-end execution} \rightarrow \text{mutual cancellation} \rightarrow \text{one transformed product} }

The likely class and address

This cluster belongs to the class:

\boxed{\text{terminal regenerative closure}}

It is not an ordinary interval-generation step. It acts at the boundary where:

two opposed orientations become one operation;

the source and generated voice exchange direction;

octave/register identity changes;

the former pair is extinguished;

a new child/product appears.

Within Maier’s six-unit terminal wheel, its local address is:

\boxed{4\leftrightarrow1}

—that is, XLIX–L, the third antipodal pair. In your larger hierarchy, that corresponds most naturally to:

\boxed{C_3\rightarrow C_4}

or:

\boxed{\text{class 3, unit 3, address 9} \rightarrow \text{class 4, unit 1, address 10}}

So Maier’s terminal pair appears to describe the same exceptional transition you identified as:

\boxed{\text{Water III}\rightarrow\text{Earth I}}

and therefore:

\boxed{\text{Yesod}\rightarrow\text{Malkuth}}

Bottom line

The best new result is not simply that XLVI says “reciprocal.” It is that Maier took Farmer 34–39 and deliberately—or at minimum exactly—arranged them as the three diameters of a six-position wheel:

\boxed{2\leftrightarrow5,\quad6\leftrightarrow3,\quad4\leftrightarrow1}

The last diameter then carries:

\boxed{ \text{three fathers} +\text{turned voices} +\text{beginning/end opposition} +\text{mutual death} =\text{new product} }

That gives us a precise first-edition target: verify Farmer 34–39 against Maier XLV–L and determine whether the printed rubrics themselves were copied unchanged. If they were, Maier’s message lies in the permutation and emblem assignment—not in alterations to the notes. The scholarly concordance confirms the source mapping, but the final evidentiary promotion still depends on side-by-side examination of the 1591 and 1617 originals. Farmer–Maier concordance

